==================================================
9. HUMAN
==================================================

The Human is the center and final authority of the ecosystem.

The Human may:

- Provide intent.
- Provide authorization.
- Reject actions.
- Configure preferences.
- Control memory.
- Control permissions.
- Receive results.
- Request clarification.

The architecture must preserve human agency.

==================================================
10. iRIEL
==================================================

iRiel is the human-facing intelligent interaction layer.

Responsibilities:

- Receive human interaction.
- Present information.
- Translate interaction into system-understandable intent.
- Present system results.
- Adapt interaction modalities.

iRiel must not directly bypass:

- Governance.
- Privacy & Security.
- Router policies.
- System authorization.

==================================================
11. CORE AI
==================================================

Core AI is responsible for:

- Understanding intent.
- Reasoning.
- Planning.
- Task decomposition.
- Context interpretation.
- Capability coordination.
- Result interpretation.

Core AI may request capabilities.

Core AI must not directly:

- Access private subsystem databases.
- Execute system actions without authorization.
- Modify Governance.
- Override Privacy & Security.
- Select providers by bypassing the Router.
- Claim execution success without verification.

Canonical rule:

Core AI determines WHAT needs to happen.

==================================================
12. CONTEXT
==================================================

Context manages information relevant to the current situation.

Responsibilities:

- Current interaction state.
- Task state.
- Environment state.
- Relevant temporary information.
- Current requirements.

Context must not automatically become permanent Memory.

Context may request information from Memory through an explicit contract.

==================================================
13. MEMORY
==================================================

Memory manages authorized continuity.

Responsibilities:

- Store authorized memory.
- Retrieve relevant memory.
- Update memory.
- Delete memory.
- Apply memory lifecycle rules.

Memory must not become:

- A universal database.
- A model provider.
- A system execution layer.
- A Governance authority.
- An unrestricted data repository.

Canonical distinction:

Context answers:

"What is relevant now?"

Memory answers:

"What should remain available for future continuity?"

==================================================
14. KNOWLEDGE
==================================================

Knowledge provides information used by the intelligence architecture.

Responsibilities:

- Store or expose knowledge.
- Retrieve information.
- Provide provenance where applicable.
- Support information grounding.
- Support domain-specific knowledge.

Knowledge must not assume responsibility for:

- User memory.
- System execution.
- AI routing.
- Governance.
- Privacy policy.

Canonical distinction:

Memory primarily represents authorized user continuity.

Knowledge primarily represents information available for reasoning and retrieval.

==================================================
15. INTELLIGENCE ROUTER
==================================================

The Router determines which capability should process a request.

Responsibilities:

- Capability discovery.
- Candidate selection.
- Routing.
- Fallback.
- Availability evaluation.
- Provider selection.
- Routing policy application.

The Router must consider:

- Intent.
- Context.
- Capability match.
- Privacy.
- Security.
- Governance.
- Latency.
- Cost.
- Reliability.
- Availability.

The Router must not:

- Execute system actions directly.
- Modify Memory directly.
- Override Governance.
- Modify Privacy policies.
- Depend on provider-specific internal APIs.
- Assume successful execution.

Canonical rule:

Core AI determines WHAT needs to happen.

Router determines WHERE and WITH WHICH CAPABILITY it should happen.

==================================================
16. CAPABILITIES
==================================================

Capabilities provide specialized functionality.

Examples:

- Vision.
- OCR.
- Translation.
- Search.
- Calculation.
- Speech.
- Messaging.
- Calendar.
- Files.
- Automation.

Capabilities must expose explicit contracts.

A capability should define:

- Identity.
- Purpose.
- Inputs.
- Outputs.
- Permissions.
- Privacy requirements.
- Security requirements.
- Supported environments.
- Failure states.
- Verification mechanism.

==================================================
17. INTEGRATIONS
==================================================

Integrations connect capabilities to external services.

Examples:

- Messaging providers.
- Cloud services.
- Productivity systems.
- Search providers.
- External APIs.
- Automation platforms.

The Router should not need to know the internal implementation of an integration.

Preferred:

Router
↓
Messaging Capability
↓
Integration Adapter
↓
External Service

Not:

Router
↓
External Service SDK

==================================================
18. PROVIDER ABSTRACTION
==================================================

PRSL-AI must prevent provider-specific dependencies from leaking into higher architectural layers.

Forbidden:

Core AI
↓
OpenAI SDK

Preferred:

Core AI
↓
AI Capability Contract
↓
Router
↓
Provider Adapter
↓
AI Provider

This allows providers to be replaced without redesigning PRSL-AI.

Potential providers include:

- Apple Foundation Models.
- OpenAI Models.
- Gemini Models.
- Local Models.
- Specialized Models.
- Future providers.

Providers are implementations.

They are not architectural authorities.

==================================================
19. iRIEL SYSTEM
==================================================

iRiel System is the execution boundary for authorized system operations.

Responsibilities:

- Receive authorized commands.
- Interact with operating system capabilities.
- Execute App Intents.
- Execute permitted automations.
- Manage system actions.
- Return execution state.

Core AI must not directly manipulate operating system APIs.

Preferred:

Core AI
↓
Router
↓
Capability
↓
iRiel System
↓
System Execution

==================================================
20. EXECUTION
==================================================

Execution is distinct from planning.

Planning:

Core AI
↓
"Send this message."

Execution:

iRiel System
↓
Message API
↓
Actual operation

Execution must produce an explicit state.

==================================================
21. VERIFICATION
==================================================

Verification is responsible for determining whether an operation actually succeeded.

The architecture must distinguish:

REQUESTED
ATTEMPTED
EXECUTED
VERIFIED

Example:

Core AI:
SEND_MESSAGE

iRiel System:
ATTEMPTED

Verification:
DELIVERED

Final state:

SUCCESS

The statement "done" from an AI model is not sufficient evidence of execution.

==================================================
22. PRIVACY & SECURITY
==================================================

Privacy & Security provides cross-cutting protection.

Responsibilities include:

- Data protection.
- Secure processing.
- Access control.
- Authorization enforcement.
- Sensitive-data handling.
- Secure communication.
- Local-processing preference.
- External processing controls.

Privacy & Security must not be bypassed by:

- Core AI.
- Router.
- Capabilities.
- Integrations.
- Devices.
- External providers.

Canonical principle:

Privacy is architectural.

==================================================
23. GOVERNANCE
==================================================

Governance determines what actions are permitted.

Responsibilities:

- Policy.
- Authorization boundaries.
- Capability restrictions.
- Data handling rules.
- Execution rules.
- Fallback restrictions.
- Compliance requirements.

Governance is authoritative.

A technically possible operation is not automatically an authorized operation.

==================================================
24. ACCESSIBILITY
==================================================

Accessibility is a cross-cutting architectural concern.

PRSL-AI may support:

- Voice.
- Text.
- Vision.
- Audio.
- Haptics.
- Assistive technologies.
- Alternative interaction methods.

Accessibility must not require duplication of the intelligence architecture.

Different interfaces should access the same underlying capabilities through defined contracts.

